\section{Discussion and Limitations}\label{sec:discussion}

% Opening paragraph (what the results mean for a leaderboard reader) is written from the final results.

\paragraph{What the data cannot say.}
Design is confounded with dataset: the crowd, expert, model-judge and sports data differ in models, prompts, graph and size as well as in who labels. Every statement about a design is therefore a statement about the instances studied. The two matched contrasts (same battles, same questions, different labeller) isolate the labeller on those instances only, and neither supports a general statement about crowds versus experts. The expert instance is small (six models, 80 questions), and its uncertainty is correspondingly wide.

\paragraph{Where the measurement applies.}
The real datasets cover a narrow part of the space of graph density, tie rate and size: the six-model sets are complete graphs with few models, Arena is a dense graph with many models, and tie rates run from none (basketball) to about a third (Arena crowd votes). How fragility depends on density, ties and $N$ is therefore read from the synthetic grid, where the truth is known, and from variation inside a dataset (subsamples, seasons, slices); the real data carry only the claims about whether they sit inside or outside their own same-graph reference. The rule that predicts $k^{*}$ is checked by holding out whole datasets and whole designs, and the number of units held out is small.

\paragraph{Scope of the estimator and the threat model.}
We study the Bradley--Terry maximum-likelihood leaderboard on pair-aggregated counts with ties as half a win. Elo updates, win rates, Rank Centrality, regularisation beyond the ridge used here, and the judge-and-prompt pipelines of other benchmarks are untested. Deletion is a measure of how much of the data carries a conclusion, not an action anyone can take on crowd votes; outcome flips are the effect of labelling error or of an adversary who can change labels; inserting votes of one's own choice would cost less than the flips we count and is outside the study. $k^{*}$ is an upper bound found by search, not a proven minimum: where the exact optimum is known the search matches it (\S\ref{sec:experiments}), but at the scale of Arena only the verified upper bound is available, and we do not claim that no cheaper alteration exists. The cost of a conclusion, and the equivalence margin, are defined for the first-order scale $Q$ used here and not for the leaderboard as a whole.

\paragraph{Practice.}
